# ATS Update — 2026-09-05 Entropy-collapse fix verified on a real long run

## Fix (see `docs/system_overview.md` §Continual Learner for the full writeup)
`mind/continual_learner.py`: `ENTROPY_COEFF` is no longer a flat constant in
the loss. `ContinualLearner.entropy_coeff` now adapts every update: ramps
up (multiplicatively, toward a 0.10 ceiling = 20x the floor) whenever
measured entropy is below `config_rl.ENTROPY_TARGET` (0.5 nats), relaxes
back toward the floor (`config_rl.ENTROPY_COEFF`) once entropy is healthy.
The floor is `config_rl.ENTROPY_COEFF` itself, not a separately-baked
constant, so `debug_gui.py`'s Hyperparameter Studio live-tune control still
works.

## Verification: 2000 fresh episodes, `REWARD_SCHEME_VERSION=2` unchanged

| | run 3 (pre-fix) | run 4 (post-fix) |
|---|---|---|
| mean reward | -193.36 | **+80.14** |
| median reward | -75.20 | **+80.75** |
| % episodes positive | 40.0% | **98.0%** |
| sustained collapse (>10 near-identical episodes) | yes (170+ exact 4470-tick repeats, hundreds of consecutive -1906.70/MaxTicks) | **no** — most-repeated tick count across all 2000 episodes was 16 occurrences (normal coincidence, not a lock) |
| grade mix | FAIR/BAD dominant in back half | MID ~98-100% almost throughout |

## Honest caveat — not a complete guarantee
One rough patch did occur (ep ~1229-1280 of 2000, worst single episode
-1541.85 at ep 1253). Traced it directly: coefficient hit its 0.10 ceiling
appropriately as entropy started dropping, but entropy still collapsed to
~0.0003 *within that one episode* — 15 mini-batch updates wasn't enough
time for even the maxed-out coefficient to arrest a fast in-episode slide.
**The difference from before**: the very next update after that episode
ended showed entropy back up to 0.55 and stayed healthy for the rest of
the run (700+ episodes, no recurrence). Before this fix, an entropy dip
compounded across hundreds of subsequent episodes and never recovered on
its own. After the fix, a dip is still possible but is self-limited to
roughly one bad episode rather than becoming a permanent trap. That's a
real, measured improvement, not full immunity — worth knowing if a future
run shows an occasional bad episode; it's expected behavior, not a sign
the fix isn't working, unless it starts recurring in *clusters* again.

